<worksheet xml:id="random-variables-discrete">
    <title>Random Variables of the Discrete Type (2.1)</title>
<page>
<definition xml:id="random-variable-definition">
<title>Random Variable Definition (2.1-1)</title>
    <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>S</m> be the sample space for a
      random experiment. A function <m>X</m> that assigns to each <m>s \in S</m>
      exactly one real number <m>X(s)=x</m> is called a <alert>Random Variable (RV)</alert>. The <alert>space</alert> of <m>X</m> is the set of real numbers
      <m>\{x\mid X(s)=x, s \in S\}</m>.
    </p>
    </statement>
</definition>
<p>Notation: Typically, we use capital letters to denote random variables and lower case letters to denote a value the random variable can take on. For example, <m>X</m> is a random variable and <m>x</m> is a value of <m>X</m>.</p>
   

<exercise workspace ="3in">
<statement>
    <p>
      <alert>Example:</alert> Three balls are randomly chosen without
      replacement from an urn containing <m>3</m> white, <m>3</m> red, and
      <m>5</m> black balls.
    </p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<definition xml:id="discrete-random-variable-definition">
    
    <statement>
    <p>
     Let <m>X</m> be a random variable with one-dimensional
      space <m>S</m> where <m>S</m> is a subset of the real numbers. If <m>S</m>
      contains a countable number of points (this means we can list them out in
      an order, or equivalently put them into a one-to-one correspondence with
      the positive integers) then we say <m>X</m> is a random variable of the
      <fillin characters="25"/> type, or that <m>X</m> is a <fillin characters="25"/> random variable.
    </p>
    </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
          Note that now we are calling
      <m>S</m> the space for the random variable <m>X</m>. In other words we
      will just think about the sample space in terms of the possible outcomes
      for <m>X</m>, the measurement under consideration.
    </p>
</page>
<page>
    <definition xml:id="def-pmf">
    <title>Probability Mass Function (pmf) (2.1-2)</title>
      
        <statement>
            <p>
                 For a discrete R.V. <m>X</m>, we define the <em>probability mass function (pmf) </em>, 
                 <m>f(x)</m>, of <m>X</m> by <m>f(x) = P(X=x)</m> for all <m>x</m> 
                  in <m>S</m> as a function that satisfies the following properties:
            </p>
        </statement>
   

   
      <ol>
        <li/>
        <li/>
        <li/>
      </ol>

 </definition>
    
<p> Note: We assume that if <m>x\not\in S</m> then <fillin characters="25"/>and we sometimes call <m>S</m> the <em>support</em> of <m>X</m>.</p>

