<exercise workspace="2in">
<statement>
    <p>
      <alert>Definition:</alert> In a collection of <m>N</m> objects, suppose
      that <m>N_{1}</m> are alike and <m>N_{2}</m> are alike so that
      <m>N_{1} + N_{2} = N</m>. Suppose we draw <m>n</m> at random without
      replacement. Let <m>X</m> be the number of type <m>N_{1}</m>. If we assume
      that each of the <m>\left( \begin{array}{c}N\\n\end{array} \right)</m>
      ways of selecting <m>n</m> objects from <m>N</m> has the same probability,
      then we say <m>X</m> has a      <fillin characters="35"/> distribution.
      Further, the pmf of <m>X</m> is:
    </p>
</statement>
</exercise>


    <p>
      <alert>Quick Check:</alert> Determine if the following describes a uniform,
      hypergeometric, or neither random variable <m>X</m>. If uniform or
      hypergeometric, give the pmf of <m>X</m>.
    </p>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
  <statement>
          <p>
            An urn contains <m>4</m> red and <m>5</m> white chips. You reach in
            and draw <m>2</m> chips without replacement. Let <m>X</m> be the
            number of red chips.
          </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
  <statement>
          <p>
             A coin is flipped. Let <m>X=1</m> if it is heads, <m>X=2</m> if it
            is tails.
          </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>
    </page>
    <page>
      <exercise workspace="1.5in">
  <statement>
          <p>
              A coin is flipped four times.
                            Let <m>X</m> be the number of heads.
          </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise> 
  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
  <statement>
          <p>
            There are <m>20</m> people in a class, <m>8</m> of which are
            freshman, <m>10</m> are sophomores, and <m>2</m> are juniors. Five people are selected at random
            and <m>X</m> is the number that are freshman.
          </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise> 
 

    <p>
      <alert>Displays of Distributions:</alert> A histogram is a good way to display
      the distribution of a random variable <m>X</m> (how the probabilities are distributed over all possible outcomes). List each point in the
      space of <m>X</m> on the x axis and then put a bar of height <m>f(x)</m>
      over it.
    </p>
<exercise workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
    <p>
      Make a sketch of the probability histogram for a R.V. <m>X</m> with a
      uniform distribution over <m>S=\{1,2,3,4,5\}</m>
    </p>
</statement>
</exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <p>
      The probability distribution histogram for a hypergeometric distribution
      changes depending on the values of <m>n, N_{1},</m> and <m>N_{2}</m>:
    </p>


      <image source="hyperhists.PNG" width="75%"/>
  

    <p>
      <m>\bullet</m> If we don't know how to compute the pmf we might make a
      histogram where the heights of the bars are the relative frequency of the
      outcomes in repeated trials of the experiment.
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>\bullet</m> A line graph is similar to a histogram but instead of a
      bar, just draw a vertical line above <m>x</m> up to the height of
      <m>f(x)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <image scale="0.75" source="linehist.PNG" width="75%"/>
    </p>
</page>
